ave the winner with over a 10% margin of victory. Additional tests show some anomalies in vote counts but no clear evidence of fraud affecting the election outcome.\n\n## Uploaded by\n\nvictor aparicio\n\nAI-enhanced title and description\n\nCopyright\n\n© All Rights Reserved\n\nWe take content rights seriously. If you suspect this is your content, claim it here.\n\nAvailable Formats\n\nDownload as PDF, TXT or read online on Scribd\n\n  * Download\n\n  * Save Save Evidence Against Fraudulent Votes Being Decisive i... For Later\n\n  * Share\n\n  * 0%0% found this document useful, Mark this document as useful\n\n  * 0%0% found this document not useful, Mark this document as not useful\n\n  * Print\n\n  * Embed\n\n  * Report\n\n0 ratings 0% found this document useful (0 votes)\n\n27 views 36 pages\n\n# \n\nEvidence Against Fraudulent Votes Being Decisive Inthe Bolivia 2019 Election\n\nThe document analyzes election data from Bolivia's 2019 presidential election using a statistical model to estimate fraudulent votes. The model estimates around 22,500 fraudulent votes for the winner, but removing these votes would still leave the winner with over a 10% margin of victory. Additional tests show some anomalies in vote counts but no clear evidence of fraud affecting the election outcome.\n\n## Uploaded by\n\nvictor aparicio\n\nAI-enhanced title and description\n\nCopyright\n\n© All Rights Reserved\n\nWe take content rights seriously. If you suspect this is your content, claim it here.\n\nAvailable Formats\n\nDownload as PDF, TXT or read online on Scribd\n\nGo to previous items\n\n  * Download\n\n  * Save Save Evidence Against Fraudulent Votes Being Decisive i... For Later\n\n  * Share\n\n  * 0%0% found this document useful, Mark this document as useful\n\n  * 0%0% found this document not useful, Mark this document as not useful\n\n  * Print\n\n  * Embed\n\n  * Report\n\nGo to next items\n\nDownload\n\nSave Evidence Against Fraudulent Votes Being Decisive i... For Later\n\nShare\n\nMore options\n\nFullscreen\n\n# Evidence Against Fraudulent Votes Being Decisive in\n\n## the Bolivia 2019 Election∗\n\n## Walter R. Mebane, Jr.†\n\n## November 13, 2019\n\n∗ Thanksto Alvaro Molina and Zach Gan for assistance, and to Diogo Ferrari and Kirill Kalinin\nfor comments.\n† Professor,\nDepartment of Political Science and Department of Statistics, Research Pro-\nfessor, Center for Political Studies, University of Michigan, Haven Hall, Ann Arbor, MI\n48109-1045 (E-mail: wmebane@[Link]).\nThe recent presidential election in Bolivia is controversial,1 with fraud allegations and\nviolent protests.\nA new model called eforensics2 offers evidence that fraudulent votes in the election\nwere not decisive for the result. The statistical model operationalizes the idea that\n“frauds” occur when one party gains votes by a combination of manufacturing votes from\nEven with estimated “fraudulent” votes removed, MAS has a margin of more than ten\npercent over CC.\nThe original draft of this note (everything except this paragraph) was produced on\nNovember 5, 2019. On November 13 messages from a couple of people lead me to think the\nbest formula for the counterfactual vote proportion with frauds reallocated, if all “stolen”\nvotes are credited to CC, is\n(2889359 − 2240920 − 2(22519.8 − 5295.8) − 5295.8)/(6137778 − 5295.8) =\n0.09925756(.09866303), which would put the election results below the level need to avoid a\n1\n[Link]\n2\n[Link]\n3\nFerrari, McAlister and Mebane (2018) and [Link]\n\n1\nrunoff election.\nThe “fraudulent” counts occur at 274 of the 34551 mesas for which votes are reported4\nAll of these 274 mesas are for votes counted as cast in Bolivia and not in another country\n(the data5 separate the votes coming from abroad).\nFigure 1 shows the distribution of turnout and vote proportions across mesas. The\nmesas eforensics classifies as having “fraudulent” counts are those in the upper right part\nof Figure 1(a), which shows the proportions of votes for MAS plotted against turnout\nproportions. The most notable feature of Figure 1(b), which shows the proportions of votes\nfor CC plotted against turnout proportions, is the high frequency of mesas in which CC\nreceives zero votes. A striking feature of both Figures 1(a)and 1(b) is the scattering of\nmesas in which the turnout proprtion is lower than .7. The eforensics model does not do\nmuch to respond to such places where somewhat lower participation or votes are reported.\nFigure 1(c), which plots MAS vote proportions versus CC vote proportions, shows that the\nparties tend to be strong in different places, as one expects for opposing parties.\n\n4\nDetails are in tables at the end of this paper. Key R code snippets are also shown there.\n5\n[Link]\n\n2\nFigure 1: Bolivia 2019 Presidential Election Data Plots\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nR: Highest Posterior Density intervals (https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/coda/html/HPDinterval.html)\nciteturn70view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/coda/html/HPDinterval.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 44\n"
